    "review": "The hero died during the Tengu fight. Decision 4623's accepted transition (log line 13393) records 7 to 0 HP and explicitly names Tengu in the death message; the exact final attack is not established. An earlier actionable defect is missing Shocker hazard recognition: at decision 4564 (13199), a described Shocker is diagonally adjacent, but the current tile has no effect and the bot starts another attack. The following target receipt (13202) leaves the hero stationary and reduces HP from 75 to 66, without uniquely identifying that damage source. Decision 4577 (13230) similarly holds beside another Shocker. The played Tengu telegraph helper models smoke bombs, but not the electrical machine's alternating radius-one footprint. The pinned game source establishes that footprint without requiring hidden orientation. A reduced public-evidence fixture and an isolated complete-snapshot helper probe reproduce the missed escape; these are not recovered original runtime memory or a replay. The proposed correction adds conservative Shocker avoidance to the existing boss escape checks and prevents bomb countdowns from authorizing electrical transit. Original-source regression: three expected assertion failures; candidate: four tests pass. The full Python suite passes 2,198 tests with four skips. This establishes local hazard recognition, not survival, boss victory, or a general repair of exhausted-combat fallback movement. No planner incidents were recorded; historical backlog items were not revalidated.",
    "learnings": "Treat a publicly described Shocker and all adjacent cells as electrical exposure even between visible pulses. Use existing checked escapes rather than waiting for damage to reveal the next orientation. Keep electrical exposure separate from smoke-bomb countdowns, and retain explicit refusal when no admitted immediate escape exists."
  },
